  "markdown_report": "**Evidence and directions.** Three starting insights guide this early-stage search. First, users report that broadly related items displace specific immediate matches. This motivates selecting context according to recent intent. Second, users describe stale interests persisting after more coherent recent activity. Longer history therefore needs selective retention. Third, Electronics has 12.27% sampled ranking errors and a −0.2595 tenth-percentile margin. Embedding similarities near 0.5 do not support global collapse as the primary explanation.\n\nThree literature insights suggest practical directions. NextItNet preserves sequential structure through residual dilated convolutions [1]. Its intermediate receptive fields provide context-selection options without changing backbone family. User-adaptive layer selection provides precedent for personalized computation [2]. This motivates learned context gates, although it does not validate this adaptation. Sampled-softmax analysis shows that temperature controls emphasis on difficult negatives [3]. This supports a simple objective baseline before explicit mining.\n\n**Framework.** Organize interventions by where uncertainty enters: **context selection → causal receptive-field gating; candidate discrimination → sampled-softmax temperature; sparse item identity → jointly trained title/description features**. These target stale context, weak margins, and insufficient identity respectively. None establishes functional compatibility or unmet purchasing needs from interaction data alone.\n\n**Candidate assessment.** Scores are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n| Idea | Scores | Positive assessment | Negative assessment |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Causal receptive-field gate [1,2] | 5/8/8/7/4 | Modest novelty, reusable context selection, directly targets stale interests and broad matches; may resolve confusing pairs with a small implementation. | Adaptive depth has precedents; gains and margin improvements are uncertain, and masking requires care. |\n| Temperature-controlled sampled softmax [1,3] | 2/7/5/9/3 | Established, inexpensive objective directly targets weak discrimination and supports useful ablations. | Limited novelty and weaker connection to stale interests; hard negatives can be false negatives. |\n| Scratch-trained pooled title/description fusion | 4/7/7/6/6 | Shared tokens may improve sparse identity and specific matching within one network. | Familiar combination; noisy text, preprocessing cost, and unverified compatibility limit its value. |\n\n**Selected idea: causal receptive-field gating.** At 0% progress, prioritize a small architectural intervention. Within the supplied NextItNet, collect representations after its existing residual blocks and learn positionwise weights over their receptive fields. The gate uses only causal hidden states. A final projection produces the existing feature dimension and item-scoring interface.\n\nThis explicitly targets two reported failures: stale interactions dominating current preferences, and broad relevance outranking specific recent matches. It also targets Electronics’ weak lower-tail separation through more discriminative context; improvement remains a hypothesis.\n\n**Pseudocode:** `states = causal_blocks(masked_embeddings); weights = softmax(gate(states)); features = project(sum(weights * states)); loss = unchanged_baseline_loss(features, positives, negatives)`.\n\nPreserve padding masks and final-nonpadding readout. Train one jointly optimized checkpoint per category; synchronize its predictor writer. Preserve diagnostic interfaces and measured definitions; unavailable category breakdowns remain unavailable. Keep seeds 0/42, all four routes, `GAGC_DATA_DIR`, and limits of 200 epochs and 200 batches/epoch. Select exclusively by pinned macro validation HR@10; diagnostics provide supporting evidence, not substitute metrics.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add causal, positionwise gates over the existing NextItNet residual-block representations, producing one jointly trained sequential model per category.",
    "motivation": "Address stale interests persisting despite coherent recent activity and broadly related recommendations displacing specific immediate matches. More selective context may also improve Electronics’ observed weak lower-tail positive–negative separation; this is an untested hypothesis.",
    "implementation_notes": "Start from the host’s corrected NextItNet. Project intermediate residual-block outputs to the existing feature dimension and combine them with learned softmax gates computed from causal states. Preserve masking throughout; obtain inference features from the final nonpadding index, never count minus one. Keep the baseline objective initially to isolate the architectural effect. Preserve log2feats(sequence), item_emb, predict_score(features, items), ModelView, DiagnosisProbe(view, device).run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Diagnostics must report measured metrics and definitions without inventing unavailable metadata. Update the embedded predictor writer and checkpoint configuration together. Use exactly one trained checkpoint per category, all four routes, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], trial-local artifacts, and at most 200 epochs with 200 batches per epoch. Select using the unchanged pinned macro validation HR@10. No timestamp features, external weights, independent experts, checkpoint averaging, or additional scoring models are required.",
    "pseudocode": "x = item_emb(sequence) * nonpadding_mask\nstates = []\nfor block in existing_nextitnet_blocks:\n    x = block(x) * nonpadding_mask\n    states.append(project_block_output(x))\nweights = softmax(causal_gate(concat(states)), dim='blocks')\nfeatures = sum(weights[k] * states[k] for k in blocks)\nfeatures = output_projection(features) * nonpadding_mask\ntrain_with_unchanged_baseline_loss(features, positive, negative)\n# Inference: gather features at maximum index where sequence != 0.\n# Score candidates through the existing jointly trained item-scoring interface.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "A constrained adaptation of adaptive computation to NextItNet’s existing causal receptive fields.",
      "negative": "Adaptive depth and multiscale feature gating already have substantial precedent."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 8,
      "positive": "Provides a reusable mechanism for adapting effective context length while retaining causal convolutional efficiency.",
      "negative": "The gate may select nearly identical features or overfit; benchmark improvement is unestablished."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Requires small projection and gating layers, with no new data pipeline or external model.",
      "negative": "Intermediate-state masking, causal correctness, and predictor/checkpoint consistency require careful implementation."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "NextItNet: A Simple Convolutional Generative Network for Next Item Recommendation",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.05163",
      "contributions": [
        "Uses residual dilated causal convolutions for sequential recommendation.",
        "Provides the backbone and receptive-field structure retained by the proposal."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish gains from the proposed positionwise receptive-field gate on this benchmark."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "User-adaptive layer selection for sequential recommendation",
      "link": "https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/16518",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides precedent for adapting sequential-model computation to users."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not directly validate the proposed NextItNet gate or its effect on the supplied user failures."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Wu et al. (2024): Sampled-softmax analysis",
      "link": "https://wujcan.github.io/papers/tois-ssm.pdf",
      "contributions": [
        "Explains how sampled-softmax gradients emphasize high-scoring negatives and how temperature changes that emphasis."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish improvements on the pinned four-category validation task.",
        "Increasing emphasis on difficult negatives can amplify false-negative effects."
      ]
    }
  ]
